# Effects and Future Transactions

```elixir
System.put_env("AL_MNESIA_DISTRIBUTED", "false")

System.put_env(
  "AL_MNESIA_DIR",
  Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "al_livebook_#{:erlang.unique_integer([:positive])}")
)

Mix.install(
  [{:al, github: "anoma/AL-Ex"}],
  config: [
    al: [
      transaction_programs: [:bootstrap, :package_system],
      package_channels: [{:builtin, {:priv, "packages"}}],
      package_environment: [:elixir_process],
      edge_providers: [AL.Edge.File],
      natives: []
    ]
  ]
)
```

```elixir
use AL
```

## Why effects exist

An AL transaction can change durable state atomically. Reading a file, making an HTTP request, or sending bytes over a socket happens outside that transaction and cannot be rolled back.

An effect is a durable object describing host work. AL dispatches it only after the transaction that created it commits. The host records the outcome in its own completion transaction.

```text
request transaction -> host work -> completion transaction
```

Application work caused by that outcome belongs in another transaction. `on_completion` arranges that future transaction when the effect is created.

```text
request transaction -> host work -> completion transaction -> future transaction
```

## Run an effect

Define an ordinary object method that emits a file effect and arranges what AL should do with its outcome.

```elixir
%AL{result: {:atomic, _}} =
  run(~S"""
    @file_reader
    #{
      super => object,
      ivars => [
        #{name => path},
        #{default => none, name => contents},
        #{default => none, name => error}
      ]
    }.

    file_reader >> init
    | Self Args Self |
    get Args path _,
    call_next_method Self Args Self.

    file_reader >> read
    | Self Observer Effect Future |
    get Self path Path,
    new effect #{arguments => [Path], operation => read, provider => file} Effect,
    on_completion Effect Outcome Future (read_finished Self Observer Outcome).

    file_reader >> read_finished
    | Self Observer #{status => ok, value => Contents} |
    set_slot Self contents Contents,
    get Observer pid Pid,
    = Message #{contents => Contents, event => file_read, reader => Self},
    send_elixir Pid Message.

    file_reader >> read_finished
    | Self Observer #{reason => Reason, status => error} |
    set_slot Self error Reason,
    get Observer pid Pid,
    = Message #{event => file_read_failed, reader => Self, reason => Reason},
    send_elixir Pid Message.
    """)
```

Create a file, then start the operation from a regular `run(source)` transaction.

```elixir
path =
  Path.join(
    System.tmp_dir!(),
    "al_effects_livebook_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}.txt"
  )

File.write!(path, "hello from an effect")
notebook = self()

%AL{
  handle: request_transaction,
  result: {:atomic, {bindings, _constraints, _state}},
  children: [continuation]
} =
  run(~S"""
    new process #{name => notebook, pid => HostNotebook} _.
    new file_reader #{name => guide_reader, path => HostPath} Reader.
    read Reader notebook Effect Future,
    await Future.
    """, bindings: %{"HostNotebook" => notebook, "HostPath" => path})

effect = bindings["$Effect"]
%AL{result: {:atomic, _}} = continuation
continuation.handle == bindings["$Future"]

receive do
  message -> message
after
  1_000 -> :timeout
end
```

`new effect #{provider => P, operation => O, arguments => A} Effect` creates the effect object. `on_completion Effect Outcome Future {Goals}` registers AL goals to run as a fresh transaction after completion, exposes its handle as `Future`, and binds `Outcome` to either `#{status => ok, value => Value}` or `#{status => error, reason => Reason}`. `await Future` waits after the requesting transaction commits; its outcome appears in the returned `%AL{children: [...]}`.

It does not suspend the transaction containing it. If that transaction aborts, neither the effect nor its future transaction exists.

## Inspect the modeled chain

The requesting transaction, effect, future, and completion transactions remain as objects. This cell reads their current slots without creating another AL transaction:

```elixir
branch = AL.Branch.head()

slots = fn object ->
  {:atomic, [{:slots, ^object, values}]} =
    :mnesia.transaction(fn -> AL.Object.read_slots(object, branch) end)

  values
end

future = bindings["$Future"]
effect_slots = slots.(effect)
future_slots = slots.(future)
{:atomic, execution_transaction} = continuation.result

[
  {"request transaction", request_transaction},
  {"effect", effect},
  {"effect completion transaction", effect_slots.completion_transaction},
  {"future", future},
  {"future execution transaction", execution_transaction},
  {"future completion transaction", future_slots.completed_by}
]
|> Enum.map(fn {kind, object} ->
  %{kind: kind, object: object, slots: slots.(object)}
end)
```

The effect's `completion_transaction` is reserved when the effect is requested. The future's `wait_transaction` points to it. The future's `outcome.value` names the transaction that ran its goals; `completed_by` names the later transaction that stored that outcome on the future. Every transaction object has an opaque ID; its numeric transaction time is in the `tx` slot.

## Arrange work after commit

`spawn {Goals}` arranges a fresh transaction immediately after the current transaction commits. It is useful when the future work does not depend on an effect.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  spawn (set_slot guide_reader error cleared).
  """)
```

Like an effect continuation, spawned goals are durable data. They never run inside the transaction that arranged them.

## Inspect an effect

The effect remains available for history and introspection.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  get HostEffect status completed.
  get HostEffect outcome Outcome.
  """, bindings: %{"HostEffect" => effect})
```

If Elixir itself needs the result, `AL.await_effect/2` waits on a completion event without polling AL state. AL application code can use `on_completion` to continue in another transaction, and `await Effect` to wait in the caller after commit.

## Long-lived resources

File watches, TCP listeners, and TCP connections can produce many events. Effects start, stop, connect, listen, send, or close those resources. Later host events enter AL as fresh transactions that call ordinary methods such as `receive` or `accept` on the resource object.

Define those methods on the class or the individual object. Incoming events do not require `on_completion`, because they are not the one-time outcome of an effect.

